+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- If the camera position coincides with the target, the forward vector is undefined. A guard is required [G].
+- If the forward vector is parallel to the world up vector ($[0,1,0]$), the cross product for the right vector becomes zero. A guard is required [G].
+- Coordinate system conventions vary. Source [S2, unofficial] explicitly applies a flip matrix to align with its rendering convention, while [S3, unofficial] constructs the matrix directly for OpenGL (+z backward). Implementers must check their renderer's convention [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Circular Sensor Array Initialization
-Programmatically generates a set of perspective camera sensors arranged uniformly in a circle around a central point, configured for Mitsuba 3 rendering [S2, unofficial]. It differs from the main method by instantiating specific renderer sensor objects with film properties rather than returning raw pose matrices, and by supporting arbitrary numbers of discrete sensors rather than a continuous video frame sequence [G]. It is chosen when rendering a static multi-view dataset or a light field using a specific path tracer [G]. Parameters include `num_sensors` (count), `render_res` (film resolution, default 256), `fov` (field of view in degrees, default 45.0), `center` (circle center, default `[0,0,0]`), and `radius` (distance, default 1.3) [S2, unofficial]. The transform for each sensor $i$ is constructed by translating to `center`, rotating around the Y-axis by $\theta_i = \frac{360.0}{num\_sensors} \cdot i$, and looking at the target `[0,0,0]` from `[0,0,radius]` with up `[0,1,0]` [S2, unofficial].
+Generates a set of perspective camera sensors arranged uniformly in a circle around a central point, configured for Mitsuba 3 rendering [S4, unofficial]. It differs from the basic path generation by instantiating full sensor objects with film and FOV parameters rather than just pose matrices [S4, unofficial]. Choose this when integrating with Mitsuba 3's scene graph. It uses the same circular angle calculation $\theta_i = \frac{360.0}{num\_sensors} \cdot i$ but constructs a `perspective` sensor with an `hdrfilm` of size `render_res` x `render_res` and a box filter [S4, unofficial]. Parameters include `num_sensors`, `render_res` (default 256), `fov` (default 45.0), `center` (default [0,0,0]), and `radius` (default 1.3) [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(n\_frames)$ to generate the poses, as each frame requires constant-time trigonometric and vector operations [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(n\_frames \times 16)$ for the output tensor storage [G].
-- **Performance:** Generation is instantaneous on modern hardware; rendering time depends on the downstream ray tracer and scene complexity [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple, deterministic, and produces smooth 360° orbits suitable for visualizing 3D reconstructions [G].
-- **Limitations:** The fixed Y-offset (`radius * 0.3`) may not be suitable for all scenes (e.g., if the object is tall or flat) [G].
-- **When to use:** When a smooth, automated 360-degree fly-around is needed for visualization or dataset creation [G].
-- **When not to use:** When interactive control is required (use [Orbit Camera Controls](3262-orbit-camera-controls.md)) or when a single specific view is needed (use [Camera Look-at Computation via Rotation](2696-camera-look-at-computation-via-rotation.md)) [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(n\_frames)$, as each frame requires constant-time trigonometric and vector operations [S3, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(n\_frames \times 16)$ bytes for storing the C2W matrices [S3, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Generating 120 frames is instantaneous on modern CPUs [S3, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple, deterministic, and smooth for 360° orbit videos [S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Fixed radius and tilt; does not adapt to scene geometry or occlusions [S3, unofficial]. Not suitable for dynamic scenes or interactive control [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Not given in the sources.
+The method originates from implementations in neural rendering and volume visualization pipelines, specifically for generating training data or visualization videos [S2, S3, unofficial]. It is commonly used in NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) workflows to create circular orbit views for evaluating novel view synthesis [S3, unofficial]. The specific coordinate flip matrix is associated with certain rendering conventions in Python-based graphics libraries [S2, unofficial].
 
